# Category 5, 6, 7 & 8 — Hibernate/JPA + PostgreSQL + Redis/Caching + REST API

> **SAMPLE QUESTIONS NOTICE:** Drill-down variants the interviewer should add: "Show me the entity code", "What happens when the lazy proxy is serialised?", "Why REPEATABLE_READ over SERIALIZABLE?", "How would you migrate this schema without downtime?", "What's the cache hit ratio you target and how do you measure it?", "How do you design the API for a polymorphic resource?".

---

## CATEGORY 5 — HIBERNATE AND JPA QUESTIONS

### Q5-1: Explain your entity design for the lead management system
**DIFFICULTY:** Medium
**CATEGORY TAG:** Hibernate/JPA

**ANSWER:**
The `Lead` entity is the core of the lead management system. It's mapped to the `lead` table, has UUID PK, multi-tenant via `orgId`, soft-deleted via `@WhereJoinTable`, and uses JSONB for tags.

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "lead", indexes = {
    @Index(name = "idx_lead_user_stage", columnList = "user_id,stage"),
    @Index(name = "idx_lead_user_created", columnList = "user_id,created_at")
})
@SQLDelete(sql = "UPDATE lead SET deleted_at = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP WHERE id = ?")  // soft delete on `repository.delete()`
@Where(clause = "deleted_at IS NULL")  // automatically filter soft-deleted rows from queries
public class Lead extends BaseEntity {  // BaseEntity has id, createdAt, updatedAt (see Q5-18)

    @Column(name = "business_name", nullable = false, length = 200)
    private String businessName;

    @Column(name = "owner_name", length = 150)
    private String ownerName;

    @Column(length = 254)
    private String email;

    @Column(length = 30)
    private String phone;

    @Column(length = 2048)
    private String website;

    @Column(length = 100)
    private String niche;

    @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)
    @Column(nullable = false, length = 20)
    private LeadStage stage = LeadStage.DISCOVERED;

    @Column(name = "reply_score")
    private Short replyScore;  // 0-100, SMALLINT in DB

    @Column(name = "conversion_score")
    private Short conversionScore;

    @Column(name = "urgency_score")
    private Short urgencyScore;

    @Column(name = "has_website")
    private boolean hasWebsite;

    @Type(type = "jsonb")  // Hypersistence Utils (formerly Hibernate Types)
    @Column(columnDefinition = "jsonb", nullable = false)
    private List<String> tags = new ArrayList<>();  // JSONB array

    @Column(name = "is_active", nullable = false)
    private boolean active = true;

    @Column(name = "deleted_at")
    private Instant deletedAt;  // soft delete marker

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)  // avoid N+1: fetch via @EntityGraph or join fetch
    @JoinColumn(name = "user_id", nullable = false)
    private User user;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @JoinColumn(name = "org_id", nullable = false)
    private Organization organization;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "lead", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    @OrderBy("createdAt DESC")
    private List<LeadActivity> activities = new ArrayList<>();

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "lead", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    @OrderBy("createdAt DESC")
    private List<LeadCommunication> communications = new ArrayList<>();

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "lead")
    @OrderBy("scoredAt DESC")
    private List<LeadScore> scores = new ArrayList<>();

    @OneToOne(mappedBy = "lead", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    private LeadAnalysis analysis;

    @Version
    private Long version;  // optimistic locking

    // equals/hashCode based on id (see Q5-20)
}
```

**Design decisions:**
1. **UUID PK** — globally unique, no enumeration risk, supports future sharding
2. **`@Where("deleted_at IS NULL")`** — soft delete auto-filter; `repository.delete()` triggers `@SQLDelete` (updates `deleted_at` instead of `DELETE`)
3. **`@Version`** — optimistic lock for concurrent updates
4. **`FetchType.LAZY`** on all `@ManyToOne` and `@OneToMany` — avoid loading related entities unless explicitly requested. We control N+1 via `@EntityGraph` or JPQL `JOIN FETCH`
5. **`CascadeType.ALL` + `orphanRemoval = true`** on `activities` and `communications` — deleting a lead deletes its history. We use this sparingly (only for genuinely owned relationships).
6. **JSONB for `tags`** via Hypersistence Utils (`@Type(type = "jsonb")`) — Postgres-native, GIN-indexable
7. **`@OrderBy`** — applies at the JPQL/HQL level (not DB-level); for DB-level ordering we add `@OrderColumn` (we don't — we always sort explicitly in queries)
8. **`Short` for scores** — 0-100 fits in `SMALLINT` (2 bytes); saves space vs `Integer` (4 bytes) at 10M leads scale
9. **No `@OneToMany` for `deals`** — we fetch deals via `DealRepository.findByLeadId(leadId)` rather than `lead.getDeals()` to keep the entity lean

**Why `BaseEntity` with `@MappedSuperclass`:** shared fields (`id`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt`, `version`) live in a base class; every entity extends it. DRY, consistent.

**KEY TERMS TO MENTION:**
- `@Entity`, `@Table`, `@Column(nullable, length)`, `@Index`
- `@SQLDelete` + `@Where` for soft delete
- `@Version` for optimistic lock
- `FetchType.LAZY` everywhere + `@EntityGraph` / `JOIN FETCH` for N+1 control
- `@Type(type = "jsonb")` (Hypersistence Utils) for JSONB columns
- `CascadeType.ALL` + `orphanRemoval = true` for owned relationships
- `@OrderBy` (JPQL-level) vs `@OrderColumn` (DB-level)
- `Short` for SMALLINT (saves space at scale)
- `@MappedSuperclass BaseEntity` for shared fields

**FOLLOW-UP QUESTIONS:**
1. Why `@Where` instead of `@SQLRestriction` (Hibernate 6.3+)?
2. How do you handle a soft-deleted lead referenced by a foreign key?
3. Why no `@OneToMany` for deals?

**FOLLOW-UP ANSWERS:**
1. `@SQLRestriction` is the JPA-standardised replacement for Hibernate's `@Where` (added in Hibernate 6.3 / JPA 3.2). We're on Hibernate 6.4 — we could migrate. The migration is mechanical (`@Where(clause = "...")` → `@SQLRestriction(sql = "...")`). We'll do it in the next cleanup sprint — `@Where` is deprecated. The behaviour is identical.
2. We don't use FK constraints to soft-deleted entities. The `lead_activity.lead_id` is a non-FK column (no `REFERENCES lead(id)` constraint) — soft-deleted leads' activities stay in the DB, queryable for audit, but the FK can't enforce referential integrity for soft deletes. We enforce in app code (`LeadService.findOrFail(leadId)` throws `NotFoundException` if soft-deleted). The trade-off: no DB-level protection against dangling references. We accept this because: (a) the app always queries via the service, never directly; (b) our daily reconciliation job checks for orphaned activities and alerts.
3. Three reasons: (a) **leanness** — adding `deals` would force loading on every `lead.getDeals()` call unless LAZY (we always want LAZY by default); (b) **explicit query control** — `DealRepository.findByLeadIdOrderByCreatedAtDesc(leadId, Pageable)` lets us paginate, filter, and sort explicitly; (c) **bidirectional sync** — keeping both sides in sync (`lead.getDeals().add(deal)` + `deal.setLead(lead)`) is a common bug source. We use unidirectional `Deal.lead` and query via repository when needed.

**RED FLAGS TO AVOID:**
- `FetchType.EAGER` on `@OneToMany` (N+1 disaster)
- `equals/hashCode` based on all fields (breaks lazy proxies)
- No `@Version` (no optimistic locking)
- Storing tags as comma-separated string (not queryable)

---

### Q5-2: How did you map relationships between Lead, Deal, Pipeline entities
**DIFFICULTY:** Medium
**CATEGORY TAG:** Hibernate/JPA

**ANSWER:**
Three main relationships: Lead → Deal (1:N), Lead → PipelineStage (N:1, current stage), Lead → StageTransition (1:N, audit history).

**Lead ↔ Deal (1:N, unidirectional from Deal side):**
```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "deal")
public class Deal extends BaseEntity {

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY, optional = false)
    @JoinColumn(name = "lead_id", nullable = false)
    private Lead lead;  // FK → lead(id)

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY, optional = false)
    @JoinColumn(name = "user_id", nullable = false)
    private User user;

    @Column(nullable = false, precision = 12, scale = 2)
    private BigDecimal proposedPrice;

    @Column(precision = 12, scale = 2)
    private BigDecimal finalPrice;

    @Column(nullable = false, length = 3)
    private String currency = "INR";

    @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)
    @Column(nullable = false, length = 20)
    private DealStage stage = DealStage.PROPOSAL;

    @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)
    @Column(nullable = false, length = 10)
    private DealStatus status = DealStatus.OPEN;
}
```

We don't have a `lead.getDeals()` collection — Deal references Lead, but not vice versa. To get a lead's deals: `dealRepository.findByLeadIdOrderByCreatedAtDesc(leadId, Pageable)`.

**Lead ↔ StageTransition (1:N, bidirectional for audit queries):**
```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "stage_transition", indexes = @Index(name = "idx_transition_lead_created", columnList = "lead_id,created_at"))
public class StageTransition extends BaseEntity {

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY, optional = false)
    @JoinColumn(name = "lead_id", nullable = false)
    private Lead lead;

    @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)
    @Column(name = "from_stage", nullable = false, length = 20)
    private LeadStage fromStage;

    @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)
    @Column(name = "to_stage", nullable = false, length = 20)
    private LeadStage toStage;

    @Column(length = 500)
    private String reason;

    @Type(type = "jsonb")
    @Column(columnDefinition = "jsonb")
    private Map<String, Object> metadata;
}
```

**Pipeline table — actually not an entity:** We don't have a `Pipeline` entity — the "pipeline" is a view (collection of leads grouped by stage). The pipeline is computed: `SELECT stage, COUNT(*) FROM lead WHERE user_id = ? AND is_active GROUP BY stage`. There's no `pipeline` table; the "pipeline view" is a service-level abstraction (`PipelineService.getPipelineView(userId)` returns `Map<LeadStage, List<Lead>>`).

We considered making `Pipeline` an entity (with `pipeline_stage` table, `pipeline_id` on lead, configurable stages per org) — but the stages are fixed (9 stages), and making them configurable per org was over-engineering. We kept stages as an enum and the "pipeline" as a query. If we ever do per-org configurable stages, we'd add a `pipeline_stage` table.

**Relationship rules we follow:**
1. **Unidirectional by default** — only make bidirectional if both sides are commonly navigated. `Deal → Lead` is unidirectional (we rarely ask "what deals does this lead have?" without paginating).
2. **`FetchType.LAZY` everywhere** — explicit `JOIN FETCH` or `@EntityGraph` for queries that need related data.
3. **`@JoinColumn` always** — explicit column name, not Hibernate's auto-generated `lead_id` (which would be the same, but explicit > implicit).
4. **No `CascadeType.REMOVE`** — we control deletion explicitly via `@SQLDelete` + service logic. `CascadeType.ALL` only on genuinely owned relationships (Lead → Activities, where deleting a lead should delete its history).
5. **`optional = false` on `@ManyToOne`** — generates a `NOT NULL` FK constraint at the DB level; app-level null check + DB-level constraint = defense in depth.
6. **`@OrderBy` only for default ordering** — most queries sort explicitly; `@OrderBy` is the "default if not specified".
7. **No `@ManyToMany`** — we always break it into a join entity (`StudentCourse` instead of `Student.courses`). `@ManyToMany` is hard to add metadata to (e.g. "when was this student enrolled in this course?").

**KEY TERMS TO MENTION:**
- `@ManyToOne(fetch = LAZY, optional = false)` + `@JoinColumn(name = "lead_id", nullable = false)`
- Unidirectional by default, bidirectional only when both sides commonly navigated
- `CascadeType.ALL` + `orphanRemoval = true` only for owned relationships
- No `CascadeType.REMOVE` — explicit deletion via service
- `@OrderBy` for default ordering, explicit `ORDER BY` in queries
- No `@ManyToMany` — always break into a join entity (for metadata)
- "Pipeline" is a query/view, not an entity (fixed stages enum)

**FOLLOW-UP QUESTIONS:**
1. Why no `@ManyToMany`?
2. How would you add per-org configurable pipeline stages?
3. What's the cost of `CascadeType.ALL`?

**FOLLOW-UP ANSWERS:**
1. Three reasons: (a) `@ManyToMany` is hard to add metadata to — if you later want to add "enrolled_at" to a StudentCourse relationship, you have to break the `@ManyToMany` into a join entity (migration pain); (b) `@ManyToMany` hides the join table, making it hard to query directly (e.g. "find all students enrolled in course X after date Y"); (c) the join table generated by `@ManyToMany` has no PK constraint by default (just two FKs), which can lead to duplicate rows. We always break into a join entity from day one — costs nothing, gains flexibility.
2. Add a `pipeline_stage` table (id, org_id, name, order_index, is_terminal) — replaces the enum for orgs that want custom stages. Migration: (a) add the table with default rows matching the enum for existing orgs; (b) change `lead.stage` from enum to FK to `pipeline_stage(id)`; (c) update all `LeadStage` references in code to look up by `pipeline_stage.name` per org. Backwards-compatible if we keep the enum as a "default stage set" for new orgs. We'd do this when we have enterprise customers with custom sales processes.
3. `CascadeType.ALL` includes `CascadeType.REMOVE` — deleting a parent entity deletes all children. If the cascade depth is deep (Lead → Activities → AuditLogs), one `repository.delete(lead)` could delete 100 rows. We use `CascadeType.PERSIST` + `CascadeType.MERGE` (the safe ones) + `orphanRemoval = true` (controlled removal via `lead.getActivities().remove(activity)`) — never `CascadeType.REMOVE` directly. We control deletion explicitly via service methods.

**RED FLAGS TO AVOID:**
- `@OneToMany(mappedBy = "lead", cascade = CascadeType.REMOVE)`
- `@ManyToMany` without thinking
- `FetchType.EAGER` on any `@*ToMany`
- No `@JoinColumn` name (letting Hibernate guess)

---

### Q5-3: Explain OneToMany, ManyToOne mappings in your project
**DIFFICULTY:** Easy
**CATEGORY TAG:** Hibernate/JPA

**ANSWER:**
**`@ManyToOne`** is the owning side of a relationship — it has the FK column. **`@OneToMany`** is the inverse side — `mappedBy` references the owning side's field name.

In our project:
- `Deal.lead` is a `@ManyToOne` (owning side, FK `lead_id` on `deal` table)
- `Lead.activities` is a `@OneToMany(mappedBy = "lead")` (inverse side — no FK on `lead` table)

**`@ManyToOne` (owning side):**
```java
@ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY, optional = false)
@JoinColumn(name = "lead_id", nullable = false,
            foreignKey = @ForeignKey(name = "fk_deal_lead"))
private Lead lead;
```

**`@OneToMany` (inverse side):**
```java
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "lead", cascade = {CascadeType.PERSIST, CascadeType.MERGE}, orphanRemoval = true)
@OrderBy("createdAt DESC")
private List<LeadActivity> activities = new ArrayList<>();
```

**Synchronisation helpers (for bidirectional relationships):**
```java
public void addActivity(LeadActivity activity) {
    activities.add(activity);
    activity.setLead(this);
}

public void removeActivity(LeadActivity activity) {
    activities.remove(activity);
    activity.setLead(null);
}
```

Without these helpers, the in-memory state can be inconsistent — `lead.getActivities()` has the activity but `activity.getLead()` is null. The persistence context may flush the inconsistent state. The helpers keep both sides in sync.

**Fetch strategies:**
- `@ManyToOne` — default is `EAGER`, we always set `LAZY` (EAGER on `@ManyToOne` is the classic N+1 trap — `leadRepository.findAll()` would fetch each lead's user separately)
- `@OneToMany` — default is `LAZY` (good)

**`orphanRemoval = true`:** when an activity is removed from `lead.getActivities()` (the collection), Hibernate deletes the row from `lead_activity` table. Without it, the `lead_activity.lead_id` would be set to NULL (and the row would orphan).

**`@OrderBy("createdAt DESC")`:** Hibernate adds an `ORDER BY` to the generated SQL when the collection is loaded. Note: this is JPQL-level ordering, applied in memory if the collection is already loaded. For DB-level ordering, use `@OrderColumn` (which adds an index column to the join table — we don't use this because we always sort explicitly in queries).

**`@JoinColumn(foreignKey = @ForeignKey(name = "fk_deal_lead"))`:** gives the FK constraint a name. Useful for migrations (you can `ALTER TABLE deal DROP CONSTRAINT fk_deal_lead` instead of guessing the auto-generated name). We name every FK constraint.

**KEY TERMS TO MENTION:**
- `@ManyToOne` is the owning side (FK), `@OneToMany(mappedBy = "lead")` is the inverse
- `FetchType.LAZY` everywhere (EAGER on `@ManyToOne` = N+1 trap)
- `orphanRemoval = true` deletes the row when removed from collection
- Synchronisation helpers (`addActivity` / `removeActivity`) keep bidirectional state consistent
- `@OrderBy` is JPQL-level (in-memory if collection loaded); `@OrderColumn` is DB-level
- `@JoinColumn(foreignKey = @ForeignKey(name = "fk_..."))` names the constraint (useful for migrations)

**FOLLOW-UP QUESTIONS:**
1. What if you forget `mappedBy` on the inverse side?
2. What's the difference between `orphanRemoval` and `CascadeType.REMOVE`?
3. How do you handle a self-referencing `@ManyToOne` (tree structure)?

**FOLLOW-UP ANSWERS:**
1. Without `mappedBy`, Hibernate treats both sides as owning — it creates two FK columns (`lead.activities` creates `lead_activities.lead_id` AND `Lead.activities` creates another join table `lead_activities`). Two tables, two FKs — chaos. The fix is always `mappedBy = "lead"` on the `@OneToMany` side referencing the `@ManyToOne` field name on the other side.
2. `CascadeType.REMOVE` deletes children when the parent is deleted (explicit `repository.delete(lead)` deletes activities). `orphanRemoval = true` deletes children when they're removed from the parent's collection (in-memory `lead.getActivities().remove(activity)` deletes the row on flush). They're complementary — `orphanRemoval` is for "this entity's lifecycle is bound to the parent's collection membership", `CascadeType.REMOVE` is for "delete children when parent is deleted".
3. We have a `category.parent` self-referencing `@ManyToOne`:
```java
@ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
@JoinColumn(name = "parent_id", foreignKey = @ForeignKey(name = "fk_category_parent"))
private Category parent;

@OneToMany(mappedBy = "parent")
private List<Category> children = new ArrayList<>();
```
The DB column `parent_id` references the same `category.id` — a self-FK. Careful: circular references (category A's parent is B, B's parent is A) would cause infinite recursion in JSON serialisation — we use `@JsonManagedReference` / `@JsonBackReference` or `@JsonIgnore` on one side.

**RED FLAGS TO AVOID:**
- `FetchType.EAGER` on `@ManyToOne` (N+1 trap)
- Forgetting `mappedBy` (creates a join table)
- Forgetting synchronisation helpers (in-memory inconsistency)
- `@OrderBy` vs `@OrderColumn` confusion

---

### Q5-4: What is lazy vs eager loading and how did you use it
**DIFFICULTY:** Easy
**CATEGORY TAG:** Hibernate/JPA

**ANSWER:**
**EAGER** loads the association immediately (in the same SQL or a follow-up SQL). **LAZY** loads it on first access (triggers a separate SQL when `lead.getUser()` is called).

Defaults in JPA:
- `@ManyToOne` + `@OneToOne` — EAGER (BAD default; we override to LAZY)
- `@OneToMany` + `@ManyToMany` — LAZY (good)

**Why LAZY everywhere:** EAGER on `@ManyToOne` means `leadRepository.findAll()` triggers a follow-up SELECT for each lead's user — N+1 (N leads, 1 user query each = N+1 queries). For 100 leads, that's 101 queries instead of 1.

**The N+1 trap (concrete example):**
```java
// Bad: EAGER @ManyToOne user on Lead
List<Lead> leads = leadRepository.findAll();  // 1 query
for (Lead lead : leads) {
    System.out.println(lead.getUser().getName());  // N more queries (one per lead)
}
// Total: 101 SQL queries for 100 leads
```

**Fixes:**
1. **`FetchType.LAZY`** (default for `@OneToMany`, override for `@ManyToOne`) — user is loaded only when `lead.getUser()` is called. If you don't call it, no extra query.
2. **`JOIN FETCH`** in JPQL — explicitly fetch the association in one query:
```java
@Query("SELECT l FROM Lead l JOIN FETCH l.user WHERE l.id = :id")
Lead findByIdWithUser(@Param("id") UUID id);
```
3. **`@EntityGraph`** — declaratively specify which associations to fetch:
```java
@EntityGraph(attributePaths = {"user", "organization"})
@Query("SELECT l FROM Lead l WHERE l.userId = :userId AND l.stage = :stage")
List<Lead> findByUserAndStage(@Param("userId") UUID userId, @Param("stage") LeadStage stage);
```
4. **`@BatchSize`** — when LAZY associations are accessed, fetch them in batches (e.g. `@BatchSize(size = 50)` on `Lead.user` means: when 100 leads' users are accessed, Hibernate fetches them in 2 queries of 50 users each, not 100 queries of 1 user each).

**Our convention:**
- All `@ManyToOne` and `@OneToOne` → `FetchType.LAZY`
- `@OneToMany` → LAZY (default)
- For queries that need associations, use `@EntityGraph` on the repository method (declarative, type-safe)
- Avoid `JOIN FETCH` in `@Query` strings (hard to refactor, no compile-time check) — prefer `@EntityGraph`
- `@BatchSize(size = 50)` on critical `@ManyToOne` (e.g. `Lead.user`) as a defense-in-depth against N+1 if a developer forgets `@EntityGraph`

**LazyInitializationException — the classic Hibernate pitfall:** happens when a LAZY association is accessed *after the session is closed* (e.g. in the controller after the service returned, in the JSON serialiser). Fix: (a) use `@EntityGraph` to fetch eagerly in the query; (b) use `spring.jpa.open-in-view = false` (we do — see below); (c) use DTOs (MapStruct) to materialise the data inside the `@Transactional` boundary.

**`spring.jpa.open-in-view`:** Spring Boot's default is `true` — keeps a Hibernate session open for the entire HTTP request, so LAZY associations can be fetched in the controller/view. This is a **trap** — it hides N+1 issues and adds a long-running session. We set `false` (the right default for a serious backend) and use `@EntityGraph` + DTO mapping inside the `@Transactional` service.

**KEY TERMS TO MENTION:**
- JPA defaults: `@ManyToOne`/`@OneToOne` = EAGER (override to LAZY), `@OneToMany`/`@ManyToMany` = LAZY (good)
- N+1 problem: `findAll()` + access to association = N extra queries
- Fixes: LAZY + `@EntityGraph` (preferred) + `JOIN FETCH` (in `@Query`) + `@BatchSize(size = 50)` (defense)
- `spring.jpa.open-in-view = false` — don't keep session open in controller/view
- DTO mapping (MapStruct) inside `@Transactional` — materialise data before session closes
- `LazyInitializationException` — accessing LAZY after session close

**FOLLOW-UP QUESTIONS:**
1. How do you detect N+1 in production?
2. What's the cost of `@EntityGraph`?
3. Why not just use EAGER everywhere if you always need the association?

**FOLLOW-UP ANSWERS:**
1. Three ways: (a) Hibernate statistics — `spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.generate_statistics = true` + log "N+1 queries" warnings; (b) **p6spy** — a JDBC proxy that logs every query with the calling stack trace, useful for finding which code path triggers N+1; (c) **OpenTelemetry traces** — we trace every DB query as a span; N+1 shows up as many small spans under one parent. We use (c) — p95 trace shows queries clearly; an N+1 is visible as 100 spans where there should be 1.
2. `@EntityGraph` adds an `OUTER JOIN` to the SQL — bigger result set, more memory, but one query instead of N+1. For associations we always need, `@EntityGraph` is the right call. For associations we sometimes need, keep LAZY and add `@EntityGraph` only on the queries that need it.
3. EAGER forces the association to be loaded for every query — even when you don't need it. E.g. `leadRepository.findAll()` for a list view doesn't need each lead's `communications` (potentially 100s of rows). EAGER on `communications` would load all of them — catastrophic. EAGER is the wrong default; explicit fetching via `@EntityGraph` is the right pattern.

**RED FLAGS TO AVOID:**
- `EAGER` on `@OneToMany` (catastrophic)
- Not knowing `@EntityGraph`
- Saying "we use `JOIN FETCH`" without explaining N+1
- Not knowing `LazyInitializationException`

---

### Q5-5: How did you handle the N+1 query problem
**DIFFICULTY:** Medium
**CATEGORY TAG:** Hibernate/JPA

**ANSWER:**
N+1 is when you fetch N parent entities and then trigger N follow-up queries for their associations (1 query for parents + N for children). We hit this in production twice; here's how we handle it.

**Case 1 — `Lead.user` access in a list view:**
- Symptom: `GET /api/leads?limit=20` returned p95 = 800ms (instead of expected 80ms). p6spy showed 21 SQL queries: 1 for leads, 20 for users (one per lead).
- Root cause: a developer wrote `leads.stream().map(l -> l.getUser().getName()).collect(toList())` in the service, with `Lead.user` set to LAZY (good default) but no `@EntityGraph` on the repository method.
- Fix: added `@EntityGraph(attributePaths = "user")` to `LeadRepository.findByUserIdAndStage`:
```java
@EntityGraph(attributePaths = {"user", "organization"})
@Query("SELECT l FROM Lead l WHERE l.userId = :userId AND l.stage = :stage AND l.isActive = true")
Page<Lead> findActiveByUserAndStage(@Param("userId") UUID userId, @Param("stage") LeadStage stage, Pageable pageable);
```
- Result: p95 dropped from 800ms to 60ms (1 query with JOIN instead of 21 queries).

**Case 2 — `Lead.communications` access in a detail view:**
- Symptom: `GET /api/leads/{id}` returned p95 = 1.2s (expected 200ms). Trace showed 1 query for lead, 1 for activities, 1 for scores, 1 for analysis, 50 queries for communications (one per comm — wrong!).
- Root cause: `Lead.communications` is `@OneToMany(mappedBy = "lead")` — LAZY (good). But inside the JSON serialiser, Jackson iterates `lead.getCommunications()`, and each `comm.getLead()` (the bidirectional back-reference) triggers a session flush + reload of the lead → N queries for N communications.
- Fix: (a) DTO mapping via MapStruct — the mapper extracts only the fields the DTO needs, inside the `@Transactional` boundary, never triggering lazy access in the serialiser; (b) `@JsonIgnore` on `Communication.lead` (break the bidirectional back-reference in JSON); (c) `@EntityGraph(attributePaths = "communications")` on the query if the comm list is always needed.
- Result: p95 dropped to 180ms.

**Case 3 — `CreditLedger` batch insert:**
- Symptom: monthly credit reset job ran for 12 minutes (expected 1 minute). 3,200 users × ~5 ledger entries each = 16K inserts, but each insert triggered a `SELECT` for the user (Hibernate's "verify the FK exists" behaviour).
- Root cause: `CreditLedger.user` is `@ManyToOne(fetch = LAZY)`. Hibernate by default verifies the FK on insert (extra SELECT).
- Fix: `@ManyToOne(fetch = LAZY, optional = false, foreignKey = @ForeignKey(value = ConstraintMode.NO_CONSTRAINT))` — we keep the FK in the DB (DDL has `REFERENCES user_account(id)`) but Hibernate doesn't verify it on insert. Or: batch inserts via `JdbcTemplate` for the bulk job (skip Hibernate's per-row checks).
- Result: 12 minutes → 30 seconds.

**Detection tools:**
1. **OpenTelemetry traces** — every DB query is a span; N+1 shows up as many small spans under one parent (visible in the trace UI)
2. **p6spy** (dev only) — logs every query with calling stack trace; we run it in dev to catch N+1 in unit tests
3. **Hibernate statistics** — `spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.generate_statistics = true` logs query counts per session; we enable in staging for integration tests
4. **Code review** — `for (X x : list) { x.getY().getZ(); }` is a code smell for N+1; reviewers flag it

**Prevention:**
- `@EntityGraph` on every repository method that returns a list and the caller accesses associations
- `@BatchSize(size = 50)` on `@ManyToOne` as defense-in-depth
- MapStruct DTO mapping inside `@Transactional` — materialise data before session closes
- `spring.jpa.open-in-view = false` — no lazy access in controller/view
- Unit tests that assert query count (via `@ExpectedQueryCount` custom annotation we wrote) — fail if N+1 introduced

**KEY TERMS TO MENTION:**
- N+1 symptom: 1 query for parents + N for children
- Three real cases: `Lead.user` list view, `Lead.communications` bidirectional back-reference, `CreditLedger.user` FK verification on insert
- Fixes: `@EntityGraph(attributePaths = ...)`, MapStruct DTO mapping inside `@Transactional`, `@JsonIgnore` on back-reference, `@BatchSize(size = 50)`, batch inserts via JdbcTemplate
- Detection: OpenTelemetry traces (DB spans), p6spy (dev), Hibernate statistics (staging), code review
- Prevention: `@EntityGraph` per list method, `@BatchSize` defense-in-depth, DTO mapping, `open-in-view = false`, query-count assertions in unit tests

**FOLLOW-UP QUESTIONS:**
1. How do you test for N+1 in CI?
2. What's `@BatchSize` and when does it kick in?
3. Why does `@JsonIgnore` help with N+1?

**FOLLOW-UP ANSWERS:**
1. Custom `@ExpectedQueryCount(n = 1)` annotation + JUnit extension that wraps the test in a Hibernate session, runs the test, asserts the query count. We have ~50 such tests for repository methods. Example: `@ExpectedQueryCount(n = 1) void testFindByUserAndStage() { repository.findByUserAndStage(userId, DISCOVERED, pageable); }` — if a developer removes the `@EntityGraph`, the test fails with "expected 1 queries, got 21".
2. `@BatchSize(size = 50)` is Hibernate's lazy-loading optimisation: when a LAZY collection (or `@ManyToOne` proxy) is accessed for N entities in a session, Hibernate fetches them in batches of `size` instead of one-by-one. E.g. `@BatchSize(size = 50)` on `Lead.user` + 100 leads' users accessed → 2 queries (50 users each) instead of 100 queries (1 user each). It's a fallback for when `@EntityGraph` isn't used (developer forgot or a new query path was added). We add it as defense-in-depth on every `@ManyToOne` on a hot entity.
3. `@JsonIgnore` on `Communication.lead` prevents Jackson from serialising the back-reference during JSON output. Without it, Jackson walks `lead.getCommunications() → each comm.getLead() → leads to lead.getCommunications() → ...` (Jackson handles the cycle by reference-tracking, but each `comm.getLead()` triggers a lazy load if the lead is detached → N queries). With `@JsonIgnore`, Jackson doesn't walk the back-reference — no lazy access. We pair this with DTO mapping (the DTO mapper accesses the fields we need, inside `@Transactional`, never in the serialiser).

**RED FLAGS TO AVOID:**
- "We don't have N+1" (every project has it; admit it)
- Not knowing `@EntityGraph`
- Saying "we use `JOIN FETCH`" without `@EntityGraph`
- Forgetting `spring.jpa.open-in-view = false`

---

### Q5-6: What is your approach to database transactions (@Transactional)
**DIFFICULTY:** Hard
**CATEGORY TAG:** Hibernate/JPA

**ANSWER:**
We use Spring's `@Transactional` declaratively on service methods, with explicit isolation levels and propagation per use case.

**Default (`@Transactional`):** `Propagation.REQUIRED` (join existing or start new), `Isolation.DEFAULT` (Postgres default = READ_COMMITTED), rollback on `RuntimeException` and `Error` (not on checked exceptions — common gotcha).

**When we override isolation:**
- **`REPEATABLE_READ`** for credit deductions — concurrent deductions to the same user's balance row must see a stable view. Postgres's MVCC makes REPEATABLE_READ nearly free (no extra locking) and prevents lost updates.
- **`SERIALIZABLE`** (rare) — we use it for the monthly credit reset (which writes to the same `credit_balance` row for every user) to prevent races between the reset job and user actions. The cost is retry on serialization failure — we retry 3x with `@Retryable`.
- **`READ_COMMITTED`** (default) — for everything else (CRUD operations, simple reads).

**When we override propagation:**
- **`Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW`** — for audit logging. We want the audit row written even if the main transaction rolls back. Audit service method is `@Transactional(propagation = REQUIRES_NEW)` — it starts a separate transaction that commits independently. Caveat: REQUIRES_NEW suspends the outer transaction (which can deadlock if outer holds locks the inner needs) — we use it only for audit (which only inserts, doesn't read locked rows).
- **`Propagation.SUPPORTS`** — for read methods that can run in a transaction or not. We use it for read-only queries that don't need a transaction but can join one if present.
- **`Propagation.MANDATORY`** — for service methods that must be called from within a transaction (we throw if no transaction). We use it for inner service methods that should never be called directly from a controller.
- **`Propagation.NOT_SUPPORTED` / `NEVER`** — we don't use these.

**`@Transactional(readOnly = true)`:** for read-only queries. Two benefits: (a) Hibernate disables dirty checking (faster); (b) we route to a read replica via `ReadWriteRoutingDataSource` (queries with `readOnly = true` go to a read replica, queries with `readOnly = false` go to the primary). We use this for all `GET` endpoints and reporting queries.

**`@Transactional(timeout = 30)`:** for long-running operations that might hold locks. The transaction rolls back after 30 seconds. We use it for batch operations to prevent a runaway job from holding locks forever.

**`rollbackFor` and `noRollbackFor`:** by default, Spring rolls back on `RuntimeException` and `Error` but not on checked exceptions. We use `@Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)` for service methods that throw checked exceptions (rare — we prefer unchecked). We use `noRollbackFor = BusinessException.class` for cases where a business exception (e.g. "lead not found") shouldn't roll back (the row was already there, we just don't want it). Actually, no — we always roll back on business exceptions; we don't use `noRollbackFor`.

**Self-invocation gotcha:** calling a `@Transactional` method from *within the same class* via `this.method()` bypasses the Spring proxy — the transaction annotation is ignored. Fix: (a) extract the method to a separate bean; (b) inject `self` via `@Lazy` (smelly); (c) use `AopContext.currentProxy()` (requires `@EnableAspectJAutoProxy(exposeProxy = true)`). We use (a) — extract to another service.

**Transaction propagation examples:**
```java
@Service
public class LeadService {
    @Transactional  // REQUIRED + READ_COMMITTED
    public Lead createLead(CreateLeadDto dto, AuthUser user) {
        // ...
        auditService.record("lead_created", lead.getId());  // REQUIRES_NEW inside
        return lead;
    }
}

@Service
public class AuditService {
    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
    public void record(String action, UUID resourceId) {
        // always commits, even if caller rolls back
    }
}

@Service
public class CreditService {
    @Transactional(isolation = Isolation.REPEATABLE_READ)
    public void deduct(UUID userId, int cost, String action, UUID refId) {
        CreditBalance balance = creditBalanceRepository.findByIdForUpdate(userId);  // SELECT ... FOR UPDATE
        if (balance.getAmount() < cost) throw new InsufficientCreditsException();
        balance.setAmount(balance.getAmount() - cost);
        creditLedgerRepository.save(new CreditLedger(userId, -cost, action, refId));
    }
}
```

**KEY TERMS TO MENTION:**
- Default: `REQUIRED` + `READ_COMMITTED` + rollback on `RuntimeException`
- Override per use case: `REPEATABLE_READ` for credit deduct, `SERIALIZABLE` for monthly reset (with `@Retryable`), `REQUIRES_NEW` for audit logging
- `readOnly = true` for reads → Hibernate dirty-checking disabled + routes to read replica via `ReadWriteRoutingDataSource`
- `timeout = 30` for batch operations
- `rollbackFor = Exception.class` for checked exceptions (rare)
- Self-invocation gotcha → extract to another bean or `AopContext.currentProxy()`

**FOLLOW-UP QUESTIONS:**
1. What's the cost of `REQUIRES_NEW`?
2. How do you test transaction rollback?
3. What's the difference between `REQUIRED` and `NESTED`?

**FOLLOW-UP ANSWERS:**
1. `REQUIRES_NEW` suspends the outer transaction (releases its locks) and starts a new one. Costs: (a) two DB connections held simultaneously (outer is suspended but the connection is kept — HikariCP is now holding 2 connections per request); (b) deadlock risk if outer holds locks the inner needs; (c) latency — two commit phases. We use it sparingly (audit, email send) and configure HikariCP to allow 2 connections per thread (`maximumPoolSize = 2 * maxThreads`).
2. Three ways: (a) `@SpringBootTest` + `@Transactional` test method + `assertTrue(TransactionAspectSupport.currentTransactionStatus().isRollbackOnly())` after triggering the exception; (b) `@DataJpaTest` with `@Transactional` (auto-rollback) — assert the entity is NOT in the DB after the rollback; (c) integration test with Testcontainers PG — call the service, expect exception, query the DB to verify state is unchanged. We use (c) for service-layer tests — most realistic.
3. `REQUIRED` joins the outer transaction or starts a new one — one physical transaction. `NESTED` creates a savepoint within the outer transaction — the inner can roll back to the savepoint without rolling back the outer. Postgres supports savepoints natively. We don't use `NESTED` — it's confusing and rare; `REQUIRES_NEW` (separate transaction) or `REQUIRED` (same transaction) covers our cases.

**RED FLAGS TO AVOID:**
- `@Transactional` on controllers (transaction should be in service)
- `@Transactional` on every method (read methods don't need it; use `readOnly = true`)
- `REQUIRES_NEW` everywhere (deadlock + connection exhaustion)
- Forgetting self-invocation gotcha
- Not knowing `readOnly = true` routes to read replica

---

### Q5-7: How did you implement custom JPQL queries
**DIFFICULTY:** Medium
**CATEGORY TAG:** Hibernate/JPA

**ANSWER:**
Three layers: (1) Spring Data JPA derived queries (auto-generated from method name), (2) `@Query` JPQL, (3) Specifications for dynamic queries.

**1. Derived queries (for simple cases):**
```java
public interface LeadRepository extends JpaRepository<Lead, UUID> {
    List<Lead> findByUserIdAndStageAndIsActiveTrue(UUID userId, LeadStage stage);  // JPQL auto-generated
    Optional<Lead> findByIdAndUserId(UUID id, UUID userId);
    long countByUserIdAndIsActiveTrue(UUID userId);
}
```
Limitation: method names get unwieldy for complex filters. We use derived queries for single-field filters and switch to `@Query` for multi-field.

**2. `@Query` JPQL (for complex queries):**
```java
@Query("""
       SELECT l FROM Lead l
       WHERE l.userId = :userId
         AND l.isActive = true
         AND l.deletedAt IS NULL
         AND (:stage IS NULL OR l.stage = :stage)
         AND (:niche IS NULL OR l.niche = :niche)
         AND (:tags IS NULL OR EXISTS (
             SELECT 1 FROM l.tags t WHERE t IN :tags
         ))
       ORDER BY
         CASE WHEN :sortBy = 'createdAt' THEN l.createdAt END DESC,
         CASE WHEN :sortBy = 'replyScore' THEN l.replyScore END DESC NULLS LAST
       """)
Page<Lead> findByUserWithFilters(
    @Param("userId") UUID userId,
    @Param("stage") LeadStage stage,  // nullable
    @Param("niche") String niche,  // nullable
    @Param("tags") List<String> tags,  // nullable
    @Param("sortBy") String sortBy,
    Pageable pageable);
```

Note: JPQL doesn't support `IF NULL` like SQL — we use `:param IS NULL OR ...` pattern (return all rows if param is null).

**3. Native SQL (for Postgres-specific features):**
```java
@Query(value = """
             SELECT * FROM lead
             WHERE user_id = :userId
               AND to_tsvector('english', business_name || ' ' || coalesce(owner_name,'')) @@ plainto_tsquery(:search)
             ORDER BY ts_rank(to_tsvector('english', business_name), plainto_tsquery(:search)) DESC
             """, nativeQuery = true)
Page<Lead> fullTextSearch(@Param("userId") UUID userId, @Param("search") String search, Pageable pageable);
```

**4. Specifications (for dynamic queries):**
For "build the WHERE clause based on which filters are present":
```java
public class LeadSpecifications {
    public static Specification<Lead> hasUserId(UUID userId) {
        return (root, query, cb) -> cb.equal(root.get("userId"), userId);
    }
    public static Specification<Lead> hasStage(LeadStage stage) {
        return stage == null ? null : (root, query, cb) -> cb.equal(root.get("stage"), stage);
    }
    public static Specification<Lead> hasTags(List<String> tags) {
        return tags == null || tags.isEmpty() ? null
            : (root, query, cb) -> root.join("tags").in(tags);
    }
    public static Specification<Lead> isActive() {
        return (root, query, cb) -> cb.isTrue(root.get("isActive"));
    }
}

// Usage:
Specification<Lead> spec = Specification.where(LeadSpecifications.hasUserId(userId))
    .and(LeadSpecifications.hasStage(stage))
    .and(LeadSpecifications.hasTags(tags))
    .and(LeadSpecifications.isActive());
Page<Lead> result = leadRepository.findAll(spec, pageable);
```

**5. Custom repository (for complex business logic):**
```java
public interface LeadRepositoryCustom {
    LeadStatsDto computeStats(UUID userId, Instant from, Instant to);
    List<Lead> bulkUpdateStage(List<UUID> leadIds, LeadStage newStage, UUID userId);
}

public class LeadRepositoryCustomImpl implements LeadRepositoryCustom {
    @PersistenceContext
    private EntityManager em;

    @Override
    public LeadStatsDto computeStats(UUID userId, Instant from, Instant to) {
        // Use EntityManager for criteria API or native SQL
        // ...
    }
}

public interface LeadRepository extends JpaRepository<Lead, UUID>, LeadRepositoryCustom {
    // ...
}
```

**6. Projections (DTOs from queries):**
```java
public interface LeadSummary {
    UUID getId();
    String getBusinessName();
    LeadStage getStage();
    Short getReplyScore();
}

@Query("SELECT l.id AS id, l.businessName AS businessName, l.stage AS stage, l.replyScore AS replyScore FROM Lead l WHERE l.userId = :userId")
List<LeadSummary> findSummariesByUserId(@Param("userId") UUID userId);
```

This fetches only the needed columns (not the full entity) — faster, less memory.

**KEY TERMS TO MENTION:**
- Spring Data derived queries (auto-generated from method name)
- `@Query` JPQL with `:param IS NULL OR ...` pattern for optional filters
- Native SQL (`nativeQuery = true`) for Postgres-specific features (full-text `to_tsvector`, `ts_rank`, JSONB operators)
- Specifications for dynamic queries (`Specification.where(...).and(...).and(...)`)
- Custom repository (`*RepositoryCustom` interface + `*RepositoryCustomImpl` with `EntityManager`) for complex business logic
- Projections (interface-based DTOs from queries) — fetch only needed columns

**FOLLOW-UP QUESTIONS:**
1. JPQL vs native SQL — when do you use each?
2. How do you prevent SQL injection in JPQL/native queries?
3. How do you handle dynamic ORDER BY?

**FOLLOW-UP ANSWERS:**
1. JPQL by default — portable, type-safe (the IDE validates the query against the entity), Hibernate generates the SQL. Native SQL only for Postgres-specific features (full-text, JSONB operators, window functions) or for performance tuning a specific query (we can hint to use a specific index). Trade-off: native SQL ties us to Postgres; JPQL lets us switch to MySQL/H2 if needed (we won't, but it's a thought).
2. JPQL/native queries are always parameterised — `:param` (named) or `?1` (positional). Spring Data + Hibernate bind parameters safely (no string concatenation). We NEVER concatenate user input into a query string. The one risk: dynamic ORDER BY (we can't parameterise a column name) — we use an allowlist: `private static final Set<String> ALLOWED_SORT_FIELDS = Set.of("createdAt", "replyScore", "businessName"); if (!ALLOWED_SORT_FIELDS.contains(sortBy)) throw new IllegalArgumentException();`. This is the standard pattern.
3. Two ways: (a) Pageable + Sort parameter — Spring Data generates the ORDER BY clause from `Sort.by("createdAt").descending()`. We pass `Sort.by(sortBy.split(":")[0]).descending()` (with allowlist validation). (b) Criteria API (for complex dynamic queries) — `cb.asc(root.get(sortBy))` programmatically. We use (a) for simple cases, (b) for Specifications.

**RED FLAGS TO AVOID:**
- String concatenation in queries (SQLi)
- Native SQL when JPQL would do (portability loss)
- Loading full entities when a projection would suffice (memory waste)
- Specifications when a `@Query` would do (over-engineering)

---

### Q5-8: How did you use Spring Data JPA repositories
**DIFFICULTY:** Easy
**CATEGORY TAG:** Hibernate/JPA

**ANSWER:**
Standard Spring Data JPA repositories — `JpaRepository<Entity, Id>` interface, Spring generates the implementation at runtime.

```java
@Repository
public interface LeadRepository extends JpaRepository<Lead, UUID>, LeadRepositoryCustom {

    // Derived query (auto-generated)
    Optional<Lead> findByIdAndUserId(UUID id, UUID userId);

    // @Query with @EntityGraph to avoid N+1
    @EntityGraph(attributePaths = {"user", "organization"})
    @Query("SELECT l FROM Lead l WHERE l.userId = :userId AND l.stage = :stage AND l.isActive = true")
    Page<Lead> findActiveByUserAndStage(@Param("userId") UUID userId, @Param("stage") LeadStage stage, Pageable pageable);

    // Native query for Postgres full-text
    @Query(value = "SELECT * FROM lead WHERE user_id = :userId AND to_tsvector('english', business_name) @@ plainto_tsquery(:q)", nativeQuery = true)
    Page<Lead> fullTextSearch(@Param("userId") UUID userId, @Param("q") String q, Pageable pageable);

    // Modifying query (UPDATE)
    @Modifying
    @Query("UPDATE Lead l SET l.stage = :newStage, l.updatedAt = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP WHERE l.id = :id")
    int updateStage(@Param("id") UUID id, @Param("newStage") LeadStage newStage);

    // Soft delete (via @SQLDelete on entity — repository.delete() triggers UPDATE)
    // Bulk delete
    @Modifying
    @Query("UPDATE Lead l SET l.deletedAt = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, l.isActive = false WHERE l.userId = :userId AND l.stage = :stage")
    int bulkSoftDeleteByUserAndStage(@Param("userId") UUID userId, @Param("stage") LeadStage stage);

    // Projection (DTO from query)
    interface LeadSummary {
        UUID getId();
        String getBusinessName();
        LeadStage getStage();
    }
    @Query("SELECT l.id AS id, l.businessName AS businessName, l.stage AS stage FROM Lead l WHERE l.userId = :userId")
    List<LeadSummary> findSummariesByUserId(@Param("userId") UUID userId);

    // Count
    long countByUserIdAndIsActiveTrue(UUID userId);
}
```

**`@Repository` annotation:** not strictly required (Spring Data auto-detects), but we add it for IDE clarity + exception translation (`PersistenceExceptionTranslationPostProcessor` translates JPA exceptions to Spring's `DataAccessException` hierarchy).

**`JpaRepository` vs `CrudRepository` vs `PagingAndSortingRepository`:**
- `CrudRepository` — basic CRUD (`save`, `findById`, `delete`)
- `PagingAndSortingRepository` extends CrudRepository — adds `findAll(Pageable)`, `findAll(Sort)`
- `JpaRepository` extends PagingAndSortingRepository — adds JPA-specific (`flush`, `saveAndFlush`, `findAll` with `EntityGraph`, batch operations)

We use `JpaRepository` for everything — most features, no downside.

**Custom repository composition:**
```java
public interface LeadRepository extends JpaRepository<Lead, UUID>, LeadRepositoryCustom { ... }
// Implementation:
public class LeadRepositoryCustomImpl implements LeadRepositoryCustom {
    @PersistenceContext
    private EntityManager em;
    // complex methods using EntityManager
}
```

**Query methods rules:**
- Method name → derived query: `findBy<FieldName><Operator>` (`findByIdAndUserIdAndIsActiveTrue`)
- `@Query` → custom JPQL/native
- `@Modifying` + `@Query` → UPDATE/DELETE (call `repository.flush()` or use `@Modifying(clearAutomatically = true, flushAutomatically = true)`)
- `@EntityGraph` → eager-fetch specific associations for this query only
- `Pageable` → pagination (`Page<Lead>` response includes total count, totalPages)
- `Sort` → sorting
- `Optional<T>` → safe null handling

**Caveats:**
1. Derived query method names are limited — beyond 3-4 fields, switch to `@Query`
2. `@Modifying` queries need `@Transactional` — we add it at the service layer, not on the repository method
3. `Page<>` queries do an extra `COUNT` query (the total) — for huge tables, use `Slice<>` (no count) when you only need "has next page" not total
4. `@EntityGraph` is the modern N+1 fix (replaces `JOIN FETCH` in `@Query`)
5. Don't put business logic in repositories — they're data access only. Business logic goes in services.

**KEY TERMS TO MENTION:**
- `JpaRepository<Entity, UUID>` — extends `PagingAndSortingRepository` + `CrudRepository`
- Derived query (`findBy<Field><Op>`) vs `@Query` JPQL vs native SQL (`nativeQuery = true`)
- `@Modifying` for UPDATE/DELETE (with `clearAutomatically` + `flushAutomatically`)
- `@EntityGraph(attributePaths = ...)` — per-query eager fetching (modern N+1 fix)
- `Page<>` (with count) vs `Slice<>` (no count, faster for huge tables)
- Projections (interface-based DTOs from queries)
- Custom repository composition (`*RepositoryCustom` + `*RepositoryCustomImpl` with `EntityManager`)
- `@Repository` for exception translation (`PersistenceException` → `DataAccessException`)

**FOLLOW-UP QUESTIONS:**
1. What's the cost of `Page<>` vs `Slice<>`?
2. How do you handle a bulk insert (10K rows)?
3. What if your derived query method name is too long?

**FOLLOW-UP ANSWERS:**
1. `Page<>` does a follow-up `SELECT COUNT(*)` to compute `totalPages`. For a query that returns 1M rows, this is another full scan (same WHERE clause). `Slice<>` skips the count — returns `hasNext` (boolean) based on whether the next page exists (fetches `limit + 1` rows, returns `limit`, `hasNext = (fetched > limit)`). For infinite scroll UIs, `Slice<>` is much faster. For paginated UIs that show "Page 1 of 100", you need `Page<>`. We use `Slice<>` for the leads list (infinite scroll), `Page<>` for admin (paginated).
2. Spring Data's `saveAll()` does row-by-row insert (N INSERT statements). For 10K rows, that's 10K round-trips. We use `JdbcTemplate.batchUpdate()` with a PreparedStatement batch (10 batches of 1000 rows = 10 round-trips) — 1000x faster. We expose this via `LeadRepositoryCustomImpl.bulkInsert(List<Lead> leads)`. Spring Boot 3.2 also supports Hibernate's batch insert via `spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.jdbc.batch_size=50` + `spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.order_inserts=true` (Hibernate groups inserts of the same entity into batches).
3. We have a rule: if a method name exceeds ~5 fields, switch to `@Query("SELECT l FROM Lead l WHERE ...")` with explicit params. The method name becomes a maintenance burden (one field rename = rename the whole method) and the IDE doesn't always refactor method names cleanly. `@Query` is more explicit and easier to read.

**RED FLAGS TO AVOID:**
- `CrudRepository` (use `JpaRepository` for more features)
- Business logic in repositories
- `saveAll()` for 10K+ rows (use `JdbcTemplate.batchUpdate`)
- `Page<>` for huge tables (use `Slice<>`)

---

*(The remaining Hibernate/JPA questions — Hibernate session management, pagination/sorting, Flyway migrations, second-level cache, slow query optimisation, soft deletes, optimistic vs pessimistic locking, batch processing, entity inheritance, audit fields (createdAt/updatedAt/createdBy via `@EntityListener` + `AuditorAware`), persist vs merge vs detach, circular reference serialisation, `@Version` optimistic lock, JPA Specifications for dynamic queries, `@EntityGraph` fetch optimisation, composite keys, JSONB with Hibernate, custom `UserType`, multiple data sources, read/write splitting, Testcontainers for repositories, deadlock retry logic, Hibernate interceptor for audit, `@Modifying` for UPDATE/DELETE, multi-tenancy with Hibernate filters — follow the same template. Drill on: `@PersistenceContext` vs `EntityManagerFactory`, the Hibernate session lifecycle, why `merge` returns a new instance, how `@Version` works under the hood, what happens when you `setRollbackOnly` manually.)*

