## CATEGORY 13 — TESTING QUESTIONS

### Q13-1: What is your testing strategy for AcquisitionOS
**DIFFICULTY:** Medium
**CATEGORY TAG:** Testing

**ANSWER:**
Four-layer testing pyramid: unit (most tests), slice (controller/service/repository in isolation), integration (full Spring context with Testcontainers), end-to-end (Playwright against staging). Code coverage 78% line, 64% branch.

**1. Unit tests (JUnit 5 + Mockito + AssertJ):** Pure unit tests of service methods. Services depend on repositories + other services, all mocked via `@Mock`. No Spring context (fast — 1000 tests in 10s). Example:
```java
@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class CreditServiceTest {
    @Mock CreditBalanceRepository balanceRepo;
    @Mock CreditLedgerRepository ledgerRepo;
    @InjectMocks CreditService service;

    @Test
    void deduct_throws_whenInsufficientCredits() {
        UUID userId = UUID.randomUUID();
        when(balanceRepo.findByIdForUpdate(userId))
            .thenReturn(Optional.of(new CreditBalance(userId, 5)));

        assertThatThrownBy(() -> service.deduct(userId, 10, "discover", UUID.randomUUID()))
            .isInstanceOf(InsufficientCreditsException.class);

        verify(ledgerRepo, never()).save(any());
    }
}
```

**2. Slice tests (Spring Boot Test slices):**
- `@WebMvcTest(LeadController.class)` — loads only the web layer + MockMvc; service beans are `@MockBean`. Tests request mapping, validation, status codes, JSON shape.
- `@DataJpaTest` — loads only JPA + a test DB (H2 by default, we use Testcontainers Postgres for prod parity). Tests repositories + `@Query` queries.
- `@JsonTest` — tests Jackson serialisation/deserialisation of DTOs.

Example `@WebMvcTest`:
```java
@WebMvcTest(LeadController.class)
@Import({GlobalExceptionHandler.class, SecurityConfig.class})
class LeadControllerTest {
    @Autowired MockMvc mockMvc;
    @MockBean LeadService leadService;

    @Test
    void getById_returns404_whenNotFound() throws Exception {
        when(leadService.findById(any(), any())).thenThrow(new NotFoundException("lead", "id"));

        mockMvc.perform(get("/api/leads/{id}", UUID.randomUUID()).header("Authorization", "Bearer test-jwt"))
            .andExpect(status().isNotFound())
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.error.code").value("NOT_FOUND"));
    }
}
```

**3. Integration tests (`@SpringBootTest` + Testcontainers):**
Full Spring context with real Postgres (Testcontainers), real Redis (Testcontainers), real Kafka (Testcontainers), WireMock for external APIs (Stripe, Google, Twilio, LLM). Tests the full request flow: controller → service → repository → DB, with Kafka events, with real SQL. Slower (~8s per test) but most realistic.

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = RANDOM_PORT)
@Testcontainers
class LeadDiscoveryIntegrationTest {
    @Container static PostgreSQLContainer<?> pg = new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:15");
    @Container static GenericContainer<?> redis = new GenericContainer<>("redis:7").withExposedPorts(6379);
    @Container static KafkaContainer kafka = new KafkaContainer();

    @LocalServerPort int port;
    @Autowired TestRestTemplate restTemplate;

    @Test
    void discoverLead_endToEnd() {
        // signup → login → call /api/leads/discover → wait for async job → assert lead in DB
    }
}
```

WireMock stubs the external APIs:
```java
@DynamicPropertySource
static void wireProps(DynamicPropertyRegistry registry) {
    WireMockServer stripe = new WireMockServer();
    stripe.start();
    stripe.stubFor(post("/v1/checkout/sessions").willReturn(okJson("{...}")));
    registry.add("stripe.api.url", () -> stripe.baseUrl());
}
```

**4. End-to-end tests (Playwright):** ~50 tests against staging — signup, login (password + Magic Link + Google), discover leads, generate outreach, book meeting, view pipeline, upgrade plan. Run nightly + before every prod deploy. Catches integration bugs that unit + integration tests miss (e.g. frontend sends wrong field name).

**5. Contract tests (WireMock + Pact):** We record Stripe/Google/Twilio/LLM request/response pairs as WireMock stubs. On every test run, the stubs are replayed. If the real provider changes their API, we catch it via a weekly "contract verification" job that hits the real provider with a test request and compares to the stub.

**6. Performance tests (Gatling):** 1000 virtual users for 10 minutes, mixed workload (read 70%, write 20%, AI 5%, billing 5%). Run nightly + on PR to main. SLO: p95 < 500ms, 5xx < 0.1%. We have a baseline; PRs that regress by >10% fail CI.

**7. Chaos tests:** Weekly — kill an ECS task mid-batch, verify Spring Batch resumes; kill Kafka for 1 min, verify producers buffer + consumers catch up; kill Redis for 1 min, verify app degrades gracefully (cache miss → DB).

**Test data management:** Testcontainers for DB + Redis + Kafka (fresh per test class). For shared data (e.g. a "test user" that many tests use), we have `TestDataFactory` that creates entities in the DB at test setup. We don't use production data in tests (privacy + consistency).

**Coverage gate:** SonarQube enforces 75% line coverage on new code. We're at 78% line / 64% branch. We don't enforce branch coverage (too strict for our domain — many branches are exception paths we don't unit-test).

**KEY TERMS TO MENTION:**
- Four-layer pyramid: unit (Mockito), slice (`@WebMvcTest`, `@DataJpaTest`, `@JsonTest`), integration (`@SpringBootTest` + Testcontainers), E2E (Playwright against staging)
- Unit: JUnit 5 + Mockito + AssertJ, no Spring context (1000 tests in 10s)
- Slice: `@WebMvcTest` (web layer + MockMvc), `@DataJpaTest` (JPA + Testcontainers PG), `@JsonTest`
- Integration: `@SpringBootTest` + Testcontainers (PG + Redis + Kafka) + WireMock for external APIs
- E2E: Playwright against staging, nightly + before prod deploy
- Contract: WireMock stubs for Stripe/Google/Twilio/LLM, weekly contract verification
- Performance: Gatling 1000 vusers, SLO p95 < 500ms + 5xx < 0.1%, baseline regression
- Chaos: weekly (kill task, kill Kafka, kill Redis)
- Coverage: SonarQube 75% gate on new code (we're at 78% line / 64% branch)

**FOLLOW-UP QUESTIONS:**
1. How do you test `@TransactionalEventListener`?
2. How do you test security configuration?
3. How do you handle flaky tests?

**FOLLOW-UP ANSWERS:**
1. Three ways: (a) **unit test the listener method directly** — call `listener.onApplicationEvent(event)` with a constructed event; assert the side effects (mock the dependencies); (b) **`@SpringBootTest` + `ApplicationEventPublisher`** — publish the event, assert the listener fired (via a `CountDownLatch` if async, or a `verify` on a mock); (c) **integration test the full flow** — trigger the event via the real code path (e.g. call the controller that publishes the event), assert the post-commit side effect. We use (a) for the listener's logic, (c) for the wiring.
2. Three layers: (a) `@WebMvcTest` with `@Import(SecurityConfig.class)` + `@WithMockUser(roles = "ADMIN")` — tests endpoint security in isolation; (b) full integration test with a real JWT — signup → login → call protected endpoint → assert 200; (c) security-specific tests — `ExpiredJwtTest`, `InvalidSignatureTest`, `TamperedPayloadTest` — tests `JwtAuthenticationFilter`'s handling of bad tokens. We have ~30 security-specific tests.
3. Three defenses: (a) **isolation** — Testcontainers gives a fresh DB per test class, no shared state; (b) **retry flaky** — we annotate flaky tests with `@Retry(maxAttempts = 3)` (only for genuinely flaky tests like timing-sensitive SSE); (c) **quarantine** — if a test is consistently flaky, we move it to a `@Quarantine` suite that runs weekly (not on every PR); the team has a TODO to fix it within 2 weeks. We have ~5 quarantined tests at any time.

**RED FLAGS TO AVOID:**
- No testing strategy (instant fail)
- 100% coverage target (impractical, encourages bad tests)
- Tests that hit real external APIs (flaky + slow)
- No integration tests (unit tests don't catch wiring bugs)

---

*(The remaining testing questions — unit tests for services, integration tests with Spring Boot Test, testing external APIs with WireMock, code coverage targets, testing security, contract testing, test data management, testing async (`@Async`, `CompletableFuture`), testing Kafka, testing `@Scheduled`, testing Spring Batch, mutation testing (PIT), property-based testing, snapshot testing — follow the same template. Drill on: how `@MockBean` differs from `@Mock`, why you should avoid `@SpringBootTest` for unit tests, how to test a `@TransactionalEventListener(AFTER_COMMIT)` without a real DB.)*

---

## CATEGORY 14 — BEHAVIORAL AND SITUATION QUESTIONS

### Q14-1: Describe a technical challenge you faced and how you solved it
**DIFFICULTY:** Medium
**CATEGORY TAG:** Behavioral

**ANSWER:**
**Situation:** Two months ago, Stripe started retrying our webhook endpoint aggressively — 600 webhooks in 2 minutes — and our server returned 500 for all of them, causing Stripe to retry again, creating a feedback loop. Within 5 minutes, our webhook handler was processing 0 webhooks (all returning 500), and paid users weren't getting their subscriptions activated. Customer support got 12 tickets in 30 minutes.

**Task:** I was on-call. I needed to stop the 500 storm within minutes (to stop the user impact), identify the root cause (to prevent recurrence), and ship a fix (to permanently resolve).

**Action:** Three steps:
1. **Triage (5 min):** I logged into the AWS console, saw the ECS task CPU at 95% + HikariCP at 100% utilisation + 100% 5xx on `/api/payments/webhook/stripe`. I checked CloudWatch Logs — the handler was throwing `CannotAcquireResourceException` from HikariCP. Diagnosis: connection pool exhaustion.
2. **Mitigate (10 min):** I scaled the ECS service to 8 tasks (from 4) — more tasks = more HikariCP pools = more connections. 5xx dropped to 30%. Not a fix but bought time. I also increased HikariCP `maximum-pool-size` from 20 to 30 per task via a config update (rolled out via a blue/green deploy in 5 min).
3. **Root cause + fix (next 4 hours):** I pulled the slowest webhook from the DB — it was a `checkout.session.completed` event whose processing took 28 seconds (the user's subscription activation involved a Stripe API call to fetch the customer's email, and Stripe was slow that minute). The handler held the DB connection for the full 28s, HikariCP exhausted. The fix: split the handler into "ingest" (just persist the raw webhook, return 200 to Stripe immediately) + "process" (async via Kafka consumer, per-event transaction). The Stripe API call now happens in the consumer, not in the request handler — a slow Stripe API call doesn't hold a DB connection. We also added a Resilience4j time limiter (5s) on the Stripe API call in the consumer.

**Result:** After the fix, the webhook handler returns 200 in <50ms (just an insert + Kafka send). The consumer processes events in <500ms (with the time limiter). HikariCP utilisation dropped to 30%. We added a load test that fires 1000 concurrent webhooks as a regression test — runs nightly, would have caught this in 5 minutes if we'd had it before. Postmortem published to the team wiki; we applied the "ingest + async process" pattern to all webhook handlers (Gmail, Twilio). No recurrence in 2 months.

**Reflection:** The root cause was an architecture weakness (synchronous processing of external retries) that I should have caught in code review when the webhook handler was first written. I added a checklist item to code reviews: "If this handler is called by an external service that retries on non-2xx, ensure it returns 2xx within 5s — process async if needed."

**KEY TERMS TO MENTION:**
- STAR: Situation (Stripe webhook storm), Task (stop 500s in minutes), Action (triage → mitigate → fix), Result (50ms p95, no recurrence)
- Specific numbers: 600 webhooks in 2 min, 12 support tickets, 28s slow webhook, 100% 5xx → 0%
- Triage tooling: AWS console, CloudWatch Logs, HikariCP `CannotAcquireResourceException`
- Mitigation: scale ECS to 8 tasks, increase HikariCP pool to 30
- Fix: split handler into ingest + async Kafka consumer, Resilience4j time limiter
- Prevention: load test (1000 concurrent webhooks), "ingest + async process" pattern, postmortem, code review checklist

**FOLLOW-UP QUESTIONS:**
1. What did you learn from this?
2. How did you communicate with the team during the incident?
3. What if the fix had not worked?

**FOLLOW-UP ANSWERS:**
1. Two lessons: (a) "decouple from external retry pressure" — every webhook handler should return 2xx in <5s and process async, regardless of how fast the processing is "in normal cases"; (b) "load test the failure paths" — we had a load test for normal webhook flow but not for the "external API slow" case. We now load test with WireMock stubs that introduce latency.
2. We have a `#incidents` Slack channel. I posted a brief update every 10 minutes during the incident: "00:00: detected, triaging. 00:05: scaled to 8 tasks, 5xx at 30%. 00:15: rolling out HikariCP config update. 00:20: 5xx at 0%, monitoring. 00:30: writing postmortem." The PM + customer support were in the channel — they used the updates to communicate with the affected users. After the incident, the postmortem went to the team wiki + a 30-min team retro.
3. I had a rollback plan: if the fix didn't reduce 5xx, I'd revert to the previous code (the synchronous handler) + increase HikariCP pool to 50 + add a per-IP rate limit on the webhook endpoint (return 429 if >100 req/min from one Stripe IP — Stripe would retry, but at a slower rate). The rollback would have stopped the 5xx storm within 10 min. The fix worked, so I didn't need to roll back, but I had the plan ready.

**RED FLAGS TO AVOID:**
- No specific numbers (looks invented)
- "I called my senior" (you ARE the senior)
- "We didn't have a postmortem" (looks immature)
- "It was someone else's bug" (even if true, you handled it)

---

### Q14-2: Tell me about a difficult technical decision you made
**DIFFICULTY:** Medium
**CATEGORY TAG:** Behavioral

**ANSWER:**
**Situation:** Three months in, we had to decide between using Kafka or sticking with Spring `ApplicationEventPublisher` for our internal pub/sub. The other backend engineer wanted Kafka ("future-proof"); I argued for Spring Events with Kafka-shaped events ("don't add operational cost before you need it").

**Task:** We had a 2-hour architecture meeting to decide. I needed to either convince him we didn't need Kafka yet, or be convinced we did, or find a middle ground. The decision would affect the entire codebase.

**Action:** I did three things:
1. **Quantify the cost of Kafka** — operational (broker to operate, schema registry, consumer lag monitoring, on-call), code (Kafka producer + consumer config, error handling, DLQ), latency (network round-trip vs in-process). At our scale (~50 events/sec peak), Kafka's overhead exceeded its benefit. I wrote a 1-page ADR with the numbers.
2. **Define the decision rule** — "Use Spring Events until a context publishes >1K events/sec or needs to be extracted to its own service". We're at 50 events/sec, nowhere near 1K. The rule is explicit and re-evaluated quarterly.
3. **Find the middle ground** — we dual-publish the events that *will* cross a service boundary (`audit-events`, `outreach-events`, `reply-events`) to both `ApplicationEventPublisher` (consumed in-process today) and Kafka (consumed by a shadow consumer today, ready to extract later). When we extract `ai` or `outreach` into its own service, we swap the in-process listener for the Kafka consumer with zero contract change.

**Result:** The other engineer agreed. We went with Spring Events + dual-publish for the 3 events that will eventually be cross-service. The ADR is in our wiki, re-evaluated quarterly. We saved ourselves 1-2 months of Kafka operational setup + ongoing monitoring. The decision is reversible (we can extract services with no contract change).

**Reflection:** The hardest part was *not* letting "future-proofing" win without quantifying the cost. Engineers (including me) tend to over-engineer for hypothetical futures. The discipline is to ask: "What's the *current* cost of NOT having X? What's the cost of having X today? When does the math flip?" For us, the math flips at 1K events/sec — well below that, Kafka is premature.

**KEY TERMS TO MENTION:**
- STAR: Situation (Kafka vs Spring Events), Task (decide in 2h meeting), Action (quantify cost, define decision rule, find middle ground), Result (Spring Events + dual-publish, ADR, saved 1-2 months)
- Quantified cost: broker ops, schema registry, consumer lag, network latency vs in-process
- Decision rule: "Use Spring Events until >1K events/sec or service extraction needed"
- Middle ground: dual-publish for cross-service-ready events
- ADR (Architecture Decision Record) — quarterly re-evaluation
- Reversible decision (zero contract change on extraction)

**FOLLOW-UP QUESTIONS:**
1. How do you handle disagreements with peers?
2. What if your decision turned out wrong?
3. What's the next decision you're evaluating?

**FOLLOW-UP ANSWERS:**
1. Two principles: (a) **quantify the trade-off** — write an ADR with numbers, not just opinions. Numbers settle most disagreements. (b) **find the middle ground** — most technical decisions aren't binary; there's usually a hybrid (we did dual-publish). When there's no middle ground, the senior engineer (me) makes the call, with a documented ADR + re-evaluation criteria. We've had 3 disagreements in 9 months — all resolved this way, no resentment.
2. I'd revisit the ADR. We re-evaluate quarterly — if the decision rule ("Spring Events until >1K events/sec") becomes wrong (e.g. we hit 1K events/sec in a context), we extract that context to a service with Kafka. The decision is *designed* to be reversible. The cost of being wrong: a small migration (swap in-process listener for Kafka consumer — 1 day per context). I'd rather be wrong on a reversible decision than be paralyzed by fear of being wrong.
3. Event sourcing the credit ledger (Q1-7). Today, `credit_balance` is a mutable row + append-only `credit_ledger`. The "true" answer is event-sourced: ledger is the source of truth, balance is a projection. The cost: 2 weeks of refactor + risk of bugs during the migration. The benefit: scales better at 100K users + audit + replay. We're evaluating quarterly — likely Q2 2027.

**RED FLAGS TO AVOID:**
- "I told him he was wrong" (no respect for peers)
- "We just used Kafka" (no decision process)
- "I was right, he was wrong" (every disagreement has trade-offs)
- Not having an ADR (looks undisciplined)

---

*(The remaining behavioral questions — production incident, improving a feature's performance, learning a new tech quickly, handling tech debt, code review process, feature you're proud of, disagreements on tech decisions, what you'd add with more time — follow the same template with STAR format. Drill on: the *specific* mistake you made (not "we" — own it), the moment you realised the issue, the conversation with the PM/stakeholder, what you'd do differently next time. Interviewers at Uber/Meta especially look for: (a) ownership ("I" not "we"), (b) specific numbers, (c) reflection on what you'd do differently, (d) honesty about mistakes.)*

---
# End Matter — Cheat Sheet, Common Mistakes, Questions to Ask, Handling Unknown Features

---

## Quick Reference — Cheat Sheet of All Key Annotations Used

### Spring Core
| Annotation | Purpose | Used Where |
|---|---|---|
| `@SpringBootApplication` | `@Configuration` + `@EnableAutoConfiguration` + `@ComponentScan` | Main class |
| `@Component` | Generic stereotype | Cross-cutting beans |
| `@Service` | Service layer stereotype | Service classes |
| `@Repository` | Repository stereotype + exception translation | Repository interfaces |
| `@Controller` / `@RestController` | Web layer | Controllers |
| `@Configuration` | Bean configuration class | Config classes |
| `@Bean` | Declare a bean (method-level) | Config methods |
| `@Autowired` | Inject dependency (we use constructor injection only) | (rarely explicit) |
| `@Qualifier("beanName")` | Pick a specific bean among multiple | Multi-impl scenarios |
| `@Profile("prod")` | Activate bean in a specific profile | Per-env beans |
| `@Lazy` | Inject a proxy (we avoid — circular dep smell) | (rare) |
| `@PostConstruct` | Setup after bean init | `RedisConfig` |
| `@PreDestroy` | Cleanup before bean destroy | `SseManager` |
| `@Scope("prototype")` | New instance per injection | `OutreachMessageProcessor` |
| `@ConditionalOnProperty` | Conditional bean based on a property | `@ConditionalOnProperty("feature.ai.enabled")` |
| `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` | Conditional if no other bean of this type | Fallback beans |
| `@Value("${key}")` | Inject a single property | (rare — we use `@ConfigurationProperties`) |
| `@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "stripe")` | Type-safe properties binding | `StripeProperties`, `RedisProperties` |
| `@Validated` | Validate `@ConfigurationProperties` | On properties classes |

### Spring Web MVC
| Annotation | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `@RestController` | `@Controller` + `@ResponseBody` (JSON) |
| `@RequestMapping("/api/leads")` | Base path |
| `@GetMapping`, `@PostMapping`, `@PutMapping`, `@PatchMapping`, `@DeleteMapping` | HTTP method shortcuts |
| `@PathVariable` | Extract URL path variable |
| `@RequestParam` | Extract query parameter |
| `@RequestBody` | Bind request body to a type |
| `@RequestHeader` | Extract a header |
| `@CookieValue` | Extract a cookie |
| `@Valid` | Validate the bound object |
| `@Validated` | Validate at the method level (for `@RequestParam` + `@PathVariable` validation) |
| `@ExceptionHandler` | Handle an exception (per controller) |
| `@RestControllerAdvice` | Global exception handler |
| `@CrossOrigin` | Per-method CORS (we use global `CorsConfigurationSource`) |
| `@ResponseStatus(code)` | Override HTTP status |

### Spring Validation (Bean Validation)
| Annotation | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `@NotNull` | Field is not null |
| `@NotBlank` | String is not null and not whitespace |
| `@NotEmpty` | Collection is not null and not empty |
| `@Size(min, max)` | Length/size constraint |
| `@Min`, `@Max` | Numeric range |
| `@Email` | Valid email format |
| `@Pattern(regexp)` | Match a regex |
| `@Positive`, `@PositiveOrZero` | Positive numeric |
| `@Future`, `@Past` | Date is future/past |
| `@Valid` | Cascade validation to nested object |
| `@AssertTrue` / `@AssertFalse` | Boolean assertion |

### Spring Data JPA
| Annotation | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `@Entity` | JPA entity |
| `@Table(name = "lead")` | Table mapping |
| `@Column(name = "business_name", nullable = false, length = 200)` | Column mapping |
| `@Id` | Primary key |
| `@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.UUID)` | Auto-generated UUID PK |
| `@OneToMany(mappedBy = "lead")` | Inverse side of 1:N |
| `@ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)` | Owning side of N:1 |
| `@OneToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)` | 1:1 |
| `@JoinColumn(name = "lead_id")` | FK column |
| `@JoinTable` | Join table for `@ManyToMany` (we avoid) |
| `@MappedSuperclass` | Inherited fields (no table) |
| `@Version` | Optimistic lock |
| `@OrderBy("createdAt DESC")` | JPQL-level ordering (in-memory) |
| `@OrderColumn` | DB-level ordering (extra column) |
| `@Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)` | Store enum as string |
| `@Transient` | Not persisted |
| `@Type(type = "jsonb")` | Custom Hibernate type (Hypersistence Utils for JSONB) |
| `@SQLDelete(sql = "UPDATE lead SET deleted_at = ...")` | Soft delete on `repository.delete()` |
| `@Where(clause = "deleted_at IS NULL")` | Auto-filter (deprecated — use `@SQLRestriction` in Hibernate 6.3+) |
| `@SQLRestriction(sql = "deleted_at IS NULL")` | JPA-standard replacement for `@Where` |
| `@Filter(name = "tenantFilter", condition = "org_id = :currentOrgId")` | Multi-tenant filter |
| `@FilterDef(name = "tenantFilter", ...)` | Define a filter |
| `@EntityGraph(attributePaths = {"user", "organization"})` | Per-query eager fetch (N+1 fix) |
| `@Query("JPQL")` | Custom JPQL |
| `@Query(value = "...", nativeQuery = true)` | Native SQL |
| `@Modifying(clearAutomatically = true, flushAutomatically = true)` | UPDATE/DELETE query |
| `@Param("name")` | Bind JPQL named param |
| `@Transactional` | Spring's transaction demarcation |
| `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` | Read-only (faster + routes to replica) |
| `@TransactionalEventListener(phase = AFTER_COMMIT)` | Listen after commit (async) |
| `@EnableJpaRepositories` | Enable JPA + custom config |
| `@EnableJpaAuditing` | Enable `@CreatedDate` + `@LastModifiedDate` |
| `@CreatedDate` | Auto-populate `createdAt` |
| `@LastModifiedDate` | Auto-populate `updatedAt` |
| `@CreatedBy` | Auto-populate from `AuditorAware` |
| `@EntityListeners(AuditingEntityListener.class)` | Enable auditing on entity |

### Spring Security
| Annotation / Class | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `@EnableWebSecurity` | Enable Spring Security |
| `@EnableMethodSecurity` | Enable `@PreAuthorize` / `@PostAuthorize` |
| `@EnableOAuth2Client` | OAuth2 client (login) |
| `@EnableResourceServer` | JWT resource server (our API) |
| `SecurityFilterChain` | Configures the filter chain (Spring Security 6) |
| `OncePerRequestFilter` | Base for custom filters (`JwtAuthenticationFilter`) |
| `@PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")` | Method-level authorisation |
| `@PostAuthorize` | Authorisation after method (rare) |
| `@Secured("ROLE_ADMIN")` | Legacy (we use `@PreAuthorize`) |
| `@RolesAllowed("ADMIN")` | JSR-250 (we use `@PreAuthorize`) |
| `UserDetailsService` | Custom user lookup (we don't use — JWT auth) |
| `AuthenticationProvider` | Custom auth logic (we have `OtpAuthenticationProvider`) |
| `AuthenticationManager` | Authenticates an `Authentication` |
| `JwtDecoder` | Decodes + verifies JWT |
| `JwtEncoder` | Encodes a JWT |
| `OAuth2UserService<OAuth2UserRequest, OAuth2User>` | Custom OAuth2 user lookup |
| `OAuth2AuthorizedClientService` | Manages OAuth2 authorized clients (token refresh) |
| `@EnableGlobalMethodSecurity(prePostEnabled = true)` | (legacy, replaced by `@EnableMethodSecurity`) |

### Spring AOP
| Annotation / Class | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `@Aspect` | Declare an aspect |
| `@Pointcut("execution(...)")` | Define a pointcut |
| `@Before("pointcut()")` | Before advice |
| `@After("pointcut()")` | After (finally) advice |
| `@AfterReturning(value = "pointcut()", returning = "result")` | After success |
| `@AfterThrowing(value = "pointcut()", throwing = "ex")` | After exception |
| `@Around("pointcut()")` | Around (full control) |

### Spring Async + Scheduling
